+The implementation identifies an observed connection using both initial
+sequence numbers: the SYN/ACK wire ACK must equal the current opposite-side ISN
+plus one (with TCP's 32-bit wrap), and the immutable identity stores the raw
+local and remote ISNs. A timestamp-bearing pure SYN creates pending state but
+no generation; exact retransmissions reuse the same pending ISN, including
+restoring a pending entry evicted from its LRU map. A SYN/ACK that cannot match
+the current opposite-side ISN does not activate a lifetime. Once that ACK
+matches, its SYN/ACK ISN replaces any older pending value for its own side,
+allowing a reused tuple to confirm the new pair. Simultaneous open converges
+when both current ISNs are observed. Ordinary traffic can bootstrap only when
+no lifecycle or pending SYN-side state exists; that path has no handshake proof
+and uses an identity mask with neither ISN known.
+
+History is bounded. Seen SYN keys are LRU; pending sides, lifecycle,
+confirmed-pair, identity, generation-sampling, and departure maps are all
+LRU. Eviction can therefore make a handshake or sample unavailable instead of
+permanently filling a map. These maps do not provide an atomic transaction
+across lifecycle, identity, and pending-side state; code rechecks the current
+lifecycle after identity validation and fails closed when required state is
+missing. Missing or failed required lifetime state and bounded allocator
+contention/exhaustion are counted; the monitor emits one warning that names
+those failure classes.
+A missing sampling entry can restart sampling cleanly, and a missing departure
+can make upload evidence unavailable. An evicted SYN key cannot distinguish a
+later retransmission from an unseen SYN; a connection that reuses the exact
+tuple and both exact ISNs is also indistinguishable from its earlier handshake.
+Map capacity and real-entry eviction passed the [2026-10-05 capacity run](profiling/2026-10-05-tcp-lifetime-capacity/README.md).
+One pinned cross-CPU SYN/ACK race converged on one active generation; it does
+not prove every interleaving. Core tuple reuse passed the
+[2026-10-05 kernel run](profiling/2026-10-05-tcp-lifetime-core/README.md).
+TCP timestamp options are parsed before generation handling, so
+flows without timestamps cannot change lifecycle state; upload and pure-ACK
+byte accounting still runs first. Ordinary traffic seen only after capture
+attachment may bootstrap without observed handshake evidence; a missing SYN
+followed by a SYN/ACK is not confirmed. A late non-SYN wire packet carries no
+generation field and is assigned the tuple's currently active generation.
+Normal TCP TIME_WAIT/MSL and sequence protection bound that ambiguity, but this
+design cannot identify arbitrary delayed wire packets across tuple reuse.
+
+The estimator preserves signed timestamp rollover within one generation. A
+higher generation clears prior tuple calibration even when its first TSval is
+numerically lower; generation zero and delayed lower-generation records are
+ignored while that tuple remains in the estimator table. Fix 1 still only
+corrects same-flow directional aggregation. The exact packaged filter passed the
+kernel verifier and 62 core lifetime assertions on 2026-10-05, including tuple
+reuse, retransmissions, delayed records and allocator exhaustion. Capacity and
+real-entry eviction passed, and one observed cross-CPU race converged. The
+issue remains open pending broader cross-CPU ordering, CAS contention, and
+performance acceptance.
 
